  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Government and employees in the Public Service reached and adopted a binding Agreement on the payment of a salary adjustment for employees in the public service for the financial year 1 April 2021 to 31 March 2022. The agreement binds the employer and employees who are employed by the State and fall within the registered scope of the Public Service Coordinating Bargaining Council. This Agreement is a culmination of a facilitated process of negotiations between the Employer (Government) and trade union parties in the Public Service Coordinating Bargaining Council. Desirous to break the deadlock and further attempt to reach an amicable solution, the parties opted to use the services of a third party facilitator and avoid declaring a formal dispute as stipulated in the Labour Relations Act.  The wage negotiations were dealt with in terms of the constitution of the PSCBC (Council). The agreement carried a majority of 60, 33% representing approximately 739 000 employees. At the request by the Public Service Coordinating Bargaining Council to the ILO in April 2021 to facilitate a wage negotiations deadlock between employees in the Public Service and the Employer (Government), the ILO supported with the secretariat of the Bargaining Council to define the scope of work to be undertaken to provide the support.   Given the political sensitivities of the negotiations, the ILO provided some perspectives in the selection of an appropriate resource person who could be guided and assisted in the facilitation process of a negotiated settlement between the parties.  The ILO provided advisory and mediation support to the parties during wage negotiation process that was undertaken during the month of May 2021 and July 2021. The culmination of the wage negotiation process was a binding Agreement reached in July 2021.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.4.2. Number of member States with improved policies or practices to promote collective bargaining and/or workplace cooperation</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The tripartite-plus constituents in NEDLAC and other key national stakeholders have adopted and begun to operationalize a set of recommendations emanating from a process aimed at repositioning the mandate, organizational and leadership structure NEDLAC.  Informed by a NELDAC fit for purpose research study commissioned by the Executive Management of NEDLAC, a comprehensive report with concrete recommendations for the repositioning of NEDLAC was validated and adopted by the tripartite-plus constituents and other national stakeholders in June 2021.   While the report and its recommendations are still to be further presented and discussed in a number of structures, including within the NEDLAC, the Secretariat of NEDLAC has begun to institute some of the recommendations of the report as they relate to strengthening the capacity of the NEDLAC Secretariat and the drafting of a revised Strategic Plan 2022/25. In January 2021, the ILO partnered with NEDLAC and a local Institute for research and reflection on domestic and global dynamics impacting on the advancement of South African society to commission a NEDLAC fit for purpose research study in 20201. Using a scenarios building model, the study aimed to come up with a set of recommendations to strengthen and repositioning NEDLAC for the next 25 years. To this end, the ILO provided technical and advisory input into the terms of reference for the research study. The ILO collaborated with the NEDLAC Governance Task Team in providing strategic and technical guidance to the research undertaken, as well as review draft reports of the research undertaken in close collaboration with the Nedlac Governance Task Team.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.4.1. Number of member States with newly developed or strengthened institutions, mechanisms or regulatory frameworks for social dialogue, labour relations or dispute prevention/resolution that address current and emerging challenges in the world of work</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
